  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1230 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWidespread Rain and Thunderstorm Chances:\n\nMuch of the day Tuesday will remain dry, but an approaching\nshortwave trough will open the door for rain chances beginning\nTuesday night and lasting through Thursday. \n\nAs this wave shifts into the High Plains, an associated surface\nfront will gradually sag south into Missouri before eventually \nstalling somewhere across the northern or central portions of \nthe state. What happens after that is less clear. \n\nInspection of ensemble clusters does reveal increasing \nagreement among members in the evolution of the upper-level \npattern over the past 24 hours. The favored solution depicts a \nclosed upper-level cyclone digging into the southwestern CONUS \nTuesday into Wednesday before pivoting through the Midwest as a \nnorthern stream shortwave digs out of Canada on Thursday. As \nthis occurs, tropical moisture is progged to be ingested into \nthe southwest flow, resulting in a plume of rich moisture \ncontent ahead of the shortwave. Indeed, a glance at NAEFS \nEnsemble Situational Awareness Tables reveals a strong signal \nfor high moisture content in forecast PWATs and specific \nhumidity across southwest Missouri and southeast Kansas. \nAbundant moisture, warm air advection, and isentropic ascent \nwill aid in the development of showers and thunderstorms along \nand ahead of the aforementioned cold front. Given the high \nmoisture content and possibility of training convection, \nrainfall could be heavy where where these factors align. At this\npoint, however, this positioning of synoptic- scale and \nmesoscale features remains the biggest forecast challenge when \nit comes to the question of, \"How much rain am I going to get?\" \nNotably, global models have trended slightly to the west over \nthe past 24 hours with their axis of greatest QPF, which would \nmean less rain across the Missouri Ozarks. This axis will \ncontinue to wobble over the next 24-48 hours, but forecast \ntrends will be monitored. All this said, confidence in at least \nsome rain between Tuesday night through Thursday is high. Rain \nchances increase Tuesday night, are highest Wednesday afternoon,\nand wind down late Thursday. \n\nNBM 72-hr QPF Probabilities Tue-Thu:\n>1 inch: 60-90%\n>2 inch: 20-80%\n>3 inch: 10-50%\n\nOverall, the greatest probabilities listed above are along and \nnorth of the I-44 corridor, while the lower probabilities are south\nof the interstate. The WPC has outlined a large portion of the\nforecast area in a risk for heavy rainfall through this period.\nMany important details still need to be resolved at this point,\nso keep up with the forecast if you are hoping for the rain. \n\nIf the aforementioned cold front sags far enough south into the\nregion Tuesday night into Wednesday, a low-end severe\nthunderstorm threat could develop given the overlap of moisture,\ninstability, lift, and shear.\n\nCooler temperatures will move in behind this system, with near\nto perhaps even below-average temperatures by the weekend.",
